    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "26f9989f_ca0ade1e",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/render/VerticesRenderStep.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 16
      },
      "lineNbr": 99,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-07-13T20:52:33Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If we have indices from the SkVertices, we\u0027ll want to reserve indexCount vertices since that\u0027s how many final vertices will be produced by the VertState.\n\nIn subsequent CLs, it might be worth looking at either caching the vertex and index data in GPU buffers based on the SkVertices unique ID, or using memcpy to move them in bulk and then use the DrawWriter::draw() functions. Since we\u0027re transforming the points as we write them currently, there\u0027s not a lot of reason to do that now, but when we switch to having the GPU transform everything that will be important.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 99,
        "startChar": 18,
        "endLine": 99,
        "endChar": 29
      },
      "revId": "87f745964e4790e69ca412a4a7e47deef388984e",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1ccfe453_ab9fcc6f",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/render/VerticesRenderStep.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 16
      },
      "lineNbr": 99,
      "author": {
        "id": 18914
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-07-18T19:00:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ok, gotcha, that\u0027s a good point. Noted for future work. Changed vertedxCount to indexCount. Quick question - I recall seeing an example of DrawWriter::Vertices being used without the caller explicitly calling reserve(). Instead, DrawWriter calls reserve() from an append() call. Just so I understand, is it preferable/more efficient to reserve the count you will need on the front side of things just to avoid more operations being performed? Or is the reserve call here not necessary?",
      "parentUuid": "26f9989f_ca0ade1e",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 99,
        "startChar": 18,
        "endLine": 99,
        "endChar": 29
      },
      "revId": "87f745964e4790e69ca412a4a7e47deef388984e",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b3014291_22b2511c",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/render/VerticesRenderStep.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 16
      },
      "lineNbr": 115,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-07-13T20:52:33Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "When it\u0027s time to implement this, I was going back and forth between whether or not it makes sense to check `info.hasColors()` or `fHasColors`, but I think `info.hasColors()` is the safer one.\n\nUnder normal circumstances info.hasColors() \u003d\u003d fHasColors, and we should add an SkASSERT to check that. But if somehow on a release build we selected the wrong Renderer and we were using an SkVertices object with colors on a renderstep that didn\u0027t want it, we\u0027d be providing extra data that would just be misinterpreted on the GPU. If we went the other direction, checking fHasColors but had a state mismatch, we\u0027d be trying to read from the colors data array of the SkVertices when that was null, which would lead to a crash or unsafe memory access, which seems worse.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 115,
        "startChar": 10,
        "endLine": 115,
        "endChar": 33
      },
      "revId": "87f745964e4790e69ca412a4a7e47deef388984e",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
